# Linux

## Build a binary

The simplest way is to use the `oriel` CLI:

```sh
oriel build
```

This runs npm to build the frontend, embeds it into the Zig binary, and outputs `zig-out/bin/<app-name>`.

Optimization modes (all are valid; `ReleaseSafe` is the default):

```sh
oriel build -Doptimize=ReleaseFast    # faster app, slower build
oriel build -Doptimize=ReleaseSmall   # smallest binary
oriel build -Doptimize=Debug          # debug symbols, slower app
```

Run directly:

```sh
oriel run   # build and run
```

## Package for distribution

Create the packages for the target OS: `.deb`, `.rpm` and `.AppImage` on Linux, `setup.exe` on Windows, `.app` and `.dmg` on macOS:

```sh
oriel package
```

All packages are written to `zig-out/package/`.

### Debian and RPM

These are built with `nfpm` and use the metadata from `build.zig`:

```zig
.package = .{
    .id = "dev.oriel.Notes",
    .name = "Oriel Notes",
    .summary = "A simple notes app",
    .version = "0.1.0",
    .license = "MIT",
    .homepage = "https://example.com",
    .categories = "Utility;",
}
```

Packages include:
- The binary in `/usr/bin/` (or `/usr/lib/<app-name>/` with a `/usr/bin` symlink, when the package holds more executables or files: see below)
- A `.desktop` entry and icons in standard locations
- An uninstall entry for the package manager
- Auto-derived runtime dependencies based on enabled modules

### AppImage

An AppImage is a self-contained, relocatable package. It does **not** bundle GTK4 or WebKitGTK; it relies on the system having them installed. This keeps AppImages small (~10 MB) but means they only run on distros with WebKitGTK 6.0.

To run the AppImage on another machine, the system must have:
- GTK4 and WebKitGTK 6.0 development packages installed (or runtime packages if available)

### Replacing an older package

`.replaces` and `.conflicts` (deb and rpm) name packages this one takes
over, such as the app's earlier package name: installing the new package
upgrades the old one instead of failing on shared files. GhostPen uses it
for the Tauri version's `ghost-pen` package:

```zig
.package = .{
    .replaces = &.{"ghost-pen"},
    .conflicts = &.{"ghost-pen"},
},
```

### Package contents

By default a package holds the app's executable (plus its desktop entry and icons). `.contents` adds more, to every format:

```zig
const cli = b.addExecutable(.{ .name = "notes-cli", .root_module = ... });
_ = oriel.addApp(b, dep, .{
    ...
    .package = .{
        .id = "dev.oriel.Notes",
        .contents = .{
            .executables = &.{cli},
            .files = &.{.{ .path = "data/model.bin", .source = b.path("data/model.bin") }},
        },
    },
});
```

- `executables`: other executables of the build, installed next to the app's executable.
- `files`: files at a path relative to the executable's directory (`/`-separated, no `..`).
- `runtime_libraries` (default on): Oriel's runtime libraries, i.e. `libggml-cuda.so` in a `-Dggml_cuda` build (a `files` entry with that path replaces it).
- `strip` (default on): the Linux packages get stripped copies of the executables and libraries (no symbol table or debug info; the dynamic symbols stay). A ReleaseSafe app drops from about 70 MB to under 20 MB; `zig-out/bin` keeps the originals. The app's executable is stripped too, so crash traces from a packaged app show addresses rather than function names; `.strip = false` keeps the symbols.

Where they go: next to the app's executable in each format. On Debian and RPM, a package with extras installs everything into `/usr/lib/<app-name>/` and links the executables into `/usr/bin`, so each program finds its companions next to its own path; the AppImage uses `usr/bin/`, the Windows installer `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\<name>` (the uninstaller removes them), and macOS `<Name>.app/Contents/MacOS/` for executables and libraries (signed with the bundle), with other files in `Contents/Resources/` behind a symlink from `Contents/MacOS/`, since code signing allows only code in `Contents/MacOS`.

# Windows

Oriel apps use Win32 and Microsoft Edge WebView2 on Windows. You can build them **natively on Windows** or **cross-compile from Linux**; both are verified on real Windows 11, including the installer.

## Requirements

- **Zig 0.16.0**, and **Node.js** for the React, Vue and Svelte templates (`vanilla` requires no Node).
- **`WebView2Loader.dll`**: Downloaded and cached automatically by the CLI (`oriel init`, `oriel webview2`, or during `oriel build` / `oriel package`).
- **NSIS 3** (`makensis`) for the installer.
  - **No admin required (recommended):** install via `oriel setup nsis` (and `oriel setup node`), or run `oriel doctor --fix`.
  - **Via winget:** `winget install --id OpenJS.NodeJS.LTS` and `winget install --id NSIS.NSIS` (note: NSIS via winget requires administrator privileges / UAC elevation).

The WebView2 runtime itself ships with Windows 11 and current Windows 10; the installer checks for it.

## Build on Windows

```sh
oriel build
oriel package
```

The CLI automatically passes `-Dwebview2-loader=<cached path>`. `zig-out\bin` gets the app with `WebView2Loader.dll` next to it; `zig-out\package` gets the installer.

## Cross-compile from Linux

```sh
oriel build -Dtarget=x86_64-windows
oriel package -Dtarget=x86_64-windows
```

The CLI detects the Windows target and supplies the cached `WebView2Loader.dll` automatically.

## Managing WebView2Loader.dll

Use `oriel webview2` to download, verify, and cache the loader:

```sh
oriel webview2                                # fetch latest stable for all architectures
oriel webview2 --arch x64                     # fetch x64 only
oriel webview2 --version 1.0.2903.40          # pin specific NuGet package version
oriel webview2 --out ./libs                   # copy extracted DLL(s) to a folder
```

The download is verified by computing the package SHA-512 digest and matching it against the official NuGet registration catalog hash.

To disable automatic network fetching in CI or air-gapped environments, set `ORIEL_NO_WEBVIEW2_FETCH=1` or use `--no-webview2` during `oriel init`.

### Manual download

If you prefer to supply the loader manually:
1. Download the [Microsoft.Web.WebView2 NuGet package](https://www.nuget.org/packages/Microsoft.Web.WebView2/) (the `.nupkg` is a ZIP archive).
2. Extract `runtimes/win-x64/native/WebView2Loader.dll` (or `win-arm64`).
3. Pass `-Dwebview2-loader=/path/to/WebView2Loader.dll` to `oriel build` or `oriel package` (a user-passed option always wins).

## The installer

`oriel package` produces a per-user NSIS installer, `<app-name>-<version>-setup.exe`. It:

- installs to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\<name>` without administrator rights,
- adds Start Menu shortcuts and an entry in Settings → Apps,
- checks that the WebView2 runtime is installed,
- supports silent installs: `setup.exe /S`, and `Uninstall.exe /S` to remove it.

It is not code-signed, so Windows SmartScreen warns on first run; see [Code signing]($link.page('docs/signing')).

## Test under Wine

For testing the Windows build on Linux before releasing, use Wine or Proton:

```sh
scripts/wine.sh setup    # one-time setup: creates .wine-test/ and installs WebView2
cd examples/smoke
zig build -Dtarget=x86_64-windows -Dwebview2-loader=$(../../scripts/wine.sh loader)
timeout 180 ../../scripts/wine.sh run .wine-test/smoke/bin/oriel-smoke.exe --auto-quit
```

This runs the app in a headless Wine environment. See `docs/windows-testing.md` for full instructions and known limitations.

# macOS

Oriel runs on macOS with AppKit and `WKWebView`, through the Objective-C runtime ([zig-objc](https://github.com/mitchellh/zig-objc)). Build on the Mac itself (Apple Silicon or Intel) with Xcode or the command-line tools installed:

```sh
oriel build
oriel dev              # Vite hot reload, and Zig rebuild + restart
```

What works today, verified on macOS 15 (arm64): windows and multiple windows, the webview with embedded assets and the SPA fallback, sync and async IPC, events, the `oriel.window` API, JS `alert`/`confirm`/`prompt`, the security policy (CSP, navigation, capabilities), `openExternal`, dev mode, `oriel update`, and every module: tray, menu bar, dialogs, notifications, settings store, clipboard, file watching, global shortcuts, input injection, updater (incl. `.app` bundles), media server, SQLite, audio capture (incl. system audio without a driver), and whisper/llama on the GPU with Metal.

**Permissions:** input injection needs Accessibility, audio capture needs the Microphone permission (or System Audio Recording for system audio). macOS grants them per app bundle; an unbundled binary is attributed to the app that started it, so test these from an `.app`.

## App bundle and disk image

`oriel build` also writes `zig-out/<Name>.app`, and `oriel package` writes `zig-out/package/<Name>.app` and `zig-out/package/<exe>-<version>.dmg` (the app and an `Applications` link to drag it to). The bundle's `Info.plist` comes from the `.package` metadata:

- identifier, name, version and icon (`.icns`, made from the PNG icon);
- `LSMinimumSystemVersion`: the target's minimum macOS. A native build targets the Mac it's built on; to support older versions, build with e.g. `oriel package -Dtarget=aarch64-macos.13.0`;
- `CFBundleURLTypes` for `.url_schemes` (deep links);
- the Microphone and System Audio Recording usage strings when `audio_capture` is enabled.

Start the bundle with `open zig-out/<Name>.app`: notifications then go through Notification Center, permission prompts show the app's name, and Launch Services delivers deep links to it.

The bundle is ad-hoc signed, enough for the Mac that built it. To distribute it, sign it with a Developer ID and notarize it: see [Code signing]($link.page('docs/signing')).

# Native dependencies

Some features require native C libraries, opt-in behind build flags.

## SQLite vector search (`-Dsqlite_vec`)

Enable vector search in SQLite:

```sh
oriel build -Dsqlite_vec
```

Default: off. Requires `sql` to be enabled.

## Local AI (`-Dllama` and `-Dwhisper`)

```sh
oriel build -Dllama -Dwhisper
```

Default: both off. Build time is about 45 seconds cold; cached rebuilds are fast. CPU features are auto-detected; for distributable packages, specify a baseline:

```sh
oriel build -Dllama -Dwhisper -Dcpu=x86_64_v2
```

Use `-Dcpu=baseline` for maximum portability.

## GPU acceleration

### CUDA (Linux)

Compile with CUDA support for whisper and llama:

```sh
oriel build -Dllama -Dwhisper -Dggml_cuda
```

This requires:
- The CUDA toolkit (set `CUDA_PATH` or pass `-Dcuda_path=/opt/cuda`)
- An NVIDIA GPU at runtime (CPU fallback works if the library can't find a GPU)

The CUDA backend is compiled as `libggml-cuda.so` and loaded at runtime from the app's directory.

First build takes a few minutes; cached builds are fast. For distributable builds, specify a target architecture:

```sh
oriel build -Dggml_cuda -Dcuda_arch=all-major
```

Measured speedup (11 s audio clip on RTX 4070): 3.2 s on CPU → 0.8 s on CUDA.

### Metal (macOS)

Built in by default on macOS (`-Dggml_metal=false` to disable). The Metal kernel sources are embedded in the executable, so no Xcode `metal` compiler step is needed; ggml compiles them for the GPU when a model loads.

```sh
oriel build -Dwhisper
```

Measured on an Apple M1 (5.9 s clip, whisper tiny.en, incl. model load): 7.3 s on CPU → about 1 s on Metal.

### Vulkan

**Not supported yet.**

# App icon

Every app uses a single source PNG (1024×1024 recommended) configured in `build.zig`:

```zig
_ = oriel.addApp(b, dep, .{
    .name = "my-app",
    .root_source_file = b.path("src/main.zig"),
    .icon = b.path("icon.png"), // optional; defaults to Oriel brand icon
    ...
});
```

If not provided, Oriel's brand icon is used as a fallback so every app has a valid icon out of the box. The icon bytes are embedded into the app module (`oriel_app.icon_bytes`) so `src/main.zig` can pass them to `oriel.main(..., .{ .icon = app.icon_bytes, ... })`.

At build time:
- **Windows**: The PNG is converted into a multi-resolution `.ico` (16, 24, 32, 48, 64, 256) and embedded directly into the executable via a Win32 resource (`RT_GROUP_ICON`). Windows Explorer, the taskbar, window title bar, Alt-Tab, Start-menu shortcuts, and the NSIS installer/uninstaller (`DisplayIcon`) use it automatically.
- **Linux**: Distribution packages (`.deb`, `.rpm`, `.AppImage`) install the icon into the hicolor icon theme (`/usr/share/icons/hicolor/<size>x<size>/apps/<id>.png`). The window icon name is set to the application ID. For unpackaged dev runs, `zig build desktop-entry` installs the icon into `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons/hicolor/`.
- **macOS**: Converted into an Apple Icon Image (`icon.icns`) for `.app` bundles, and set dynamically on the Dock via `NSApp setApplicationIconImage:` for unbundled dev runs.
